Forkspace creates disposable project workspaces using the fastest backend the host supports.

Linux overlayfs, APFS clones, Linux reflinks, and a portable copy floor share one command and state contract. Backend selection is automatic and sticky per workspace.

Usage: forkspace <COMMAND>

Commands:
  new      Copy the project you are in into a new workspace
  drop     Throw workspaces away (and stop what runs in them)
  enter    Open a shell (or run a command) inside a workspace
  run      Start a process in a workspace (dev server, Electron, etc.)
  switch   Switch the active workspace (hot-swap via proxy)
  ls       List your workspaces: what changed, how old, what runs
  diff     Show what changed in a workspace, or compare several
  merge    Put a workspace's changes into your project (and keep its commits as a branch)
  undo     Put your project back as it was before the latest merge
  pull     Bring your project's newer changes into a workspace
  save     Save a snapshot of a workspace to go back to
  restore  Go back to a snapshot (without one, list them)
  upgrade  Upgrade forkspace to the latest release (or to a given version) [aliases: update]
  setup    Set up the coding agents on this machine (Claude Code) to use forkspace
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
